// A `static` may not have an optional type — refused AT THE DECLARATION, by the
// rule rather than by an incidental type mismatch (DF-226f, ruled Aug 17).
//
// A static is initialized once at compile time and never changes, so the one
// thing an optional buys — "not there yet, ask before using" — is a state a
// static cannot be in. Whichever of present/absent the initializer names is the
// value forever, and every read would pay an unwrap for an answer fixed at
// compile time.
//
// `static SLOT: Int? = 7` used to report ``static `SLOT` has type `Int?` but
// its initializer has type `Int` `` — true, but it names the symptom (statics
// never auto-wrapped) instead of the rule, and it invites the reader to "fix"
// it by wrapping, which then fails for a second reason. DF-226d only changed
// which WIDTH that message reported. Auto-wrap is deliberately NOT added.
//
// The check is asked on the TYPE, before the initializer is looked at, so every
// spelling meets one rule — see static_optional_type_refused_none.saw for the
// bare-`None` and no-initializer forms. It also does not bail out: the static
// still registers, so a later USE of the name resolves instead of drawing a
// second, misleading `is declared after this point`.
//
// EXPECT: error
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: static `SLOT` may not have an optional type (`Int?`)
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: initialized once at compile time
// EXPECT-ERROR-CONTAINS: declare it as the payload type

static SLOT: Int? = 7

func main() {
    print("{SLOT!}")
}
